wayland-server: a surface's view for a client that presents with Vulkan

Haiku has no Wayland Vulkan WSI (Mesa's needs linux-dmabuf); Mesa's Haiku WSI
presents into a BView (VK_EXT_headless_surface with a
VkHaikuSurfaceCreateInfoMESA). A client of this in-process server can present
its window that way if it gets the view of its wl_surface: wgpu (Photocraft's
eframe/winit window) does.

- wl_ips_haiku_surface_view(surface id), exported: the surface's BView, or
  NULL. Synchronous (a message to the server's thread, as
  wl_ips_haiku_set_mode): the client's earlier requests are handled first.
- The view's window is run (still hidden) if it was not yet: until its first
  Show() a BWindow stays locked by the thread that made it (the server's), and
  the WSI locks the window to read the view's size and to draw a frame.
- Such a surface never has a buffer: its size is its xdg window geometry (the
  view and, without server-side decorations, the window take it on each
  commit), its window is shown at the first commit with a geometry, and it is
  not invalidated on commits (nothing to draw: the view color is already
  B_TRANSPARENT_COLOR, as the WSI wants). The client commits after each frame,
  as a Wayland WSI does.

